          }
        },
        {
          "id": "gns-9",
          "type": "text",
          "data": {
            "html": "<p>When punchcutters cut 6-point pearl type by hand, they widened the x-height, opened the apertures, and thickened the hairlines so ink spread wouldn&#x27;t choke the counters. Our new variable family, Kursbuch Serif, automates that optical shift across 6 to 72 points.</p>",
            "variables": []
          },
          "style": {
            "padding": {
              "top": 0,
              "right": 40,
              "bottom": 28,
              "left": 40
            },
            "alignment": "left",
            "fontSize": 17,
            "lineHeight": 1.65,
            "color": "#111111"
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "gns-10",
          "type": "card",
          "data": {
            "heading": "Tabular figures and fractions built for schedules",
            "body": "Every weight shares identical 580-unit tabular numerals, arrows, and platform box glyphs—so switching from Regular to Bold in a data row never shifts a column by a single pixel."
